content.theory:
JavaScript runs on a single thread, so it cannot block while waiting for a timer or a network request. Instead, it uses the **event loop**: synchronous code runs first, and asynchronous tasks (timers, network calls, user events) are queued and executed later, after the current call stack empties.

### setTimeout — the async intro
```js
console.log("start");
setTimeout(() => console.log("inside timer"), 0);
console.log("end");
// start
// end
// inside timer
```
Even with a 0ms delay, "inside timer" prints last — the callback is queued for later. This is why the order can feel surprising.

### From callbacks to promises
Callback nesting gets ugly fast. A **Promise** represents a value that isn't available yet. It starts **pending**, then settles into either **fulfilled** (resolved with a value) or **rejected** (an error).
```js
const promise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  setTimeout(() => resolve("Done"), 500);
});
promise.then((value) => console.log(value));   // Done
```
`.then()` runs when the promise fulfills; `.catch()` runs when it rejects.

### async / await — the modern idiom
```js
async function run() {
  const value = await promise;
  console.log(value);   // Done
}
```
Mark a function `async`, then `await` any promise inside it. The code reads top-to-bottom like synchronous code, but never blocks the main thread. Remember: `await` only works inside `async` functions — using it elsewhere is a SyntaxError.

### Why async matters
Fetching data from an API, waiting on a timer, reading a file — these all take time you shouldn't waste blocking the UI. Async JS keeps apps responsive: `fetch(...)` returns a promise, `await` unpacks it. Common mistake: forgetting `await`, so you log a Promise object instead of its value. Ask yourself: is this a promise? Then await it.

codeSnippets.code:
const promise = new Promise((resolve) => {
  resolve("Done");
});
promise.then((value) => console.log(value));

async function run() {
  const value = await Promise.resolve("Hello");
  return value;
}
run().then(console.log);
